/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Containers Containers
 * @ingroup Foundation
 * @brief Containers the standard library does not provide.
 *
 * Containers provides a bounded FIFO over storage supplied by the caller and
 * a packed bit vector. Vectors, stacks and queues are standard types and come
 * from CPSTL (`cpstd::vector`, `cpstd::stack`, `cpstd::queue`), which aliases
 * `std` where a standard library exists and provides its own implementation
 * elsewhere, such as AVR.
 *
 * @section FoundationContainersHeaders Public Headers
 *
 * | Need | Header |
 * | ---- | ------ |
 * | Entire Foundation library | `#include <Foundation.h>` |
 * | Entire Containers module | `#include <Foundation_Containers.h>` |
 * | Nested module umbrella | `#include <Foundation/Containers.h>` |
 * | One container only | `#include <Foundation/Containers/<Container>.h>` |
 *
 * @section FoundationContainersTypes Container Types
 *
 * | Type | Ordering | Full operation | Empty operation |
 * | ---- | -------- | -------------- | --------------- |
 * | Foundation::Containers::CircularBuffer | FIFO with wraparound | `Push()` returns `false` | `Pop()` returns `false` |
 * | Foundation::Containers::BitVector | Indexed bits, eight per byte | Owned: grows; external buffer: `PushBack()` returns `false` | `PopBack()` returns `false` |
 *
 * @section FoundationContainersUsage Basic Usage
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <Foundation_Containers.h>
 *
 * int storage[4] = {};
 * Foundation::Containers::CircularBuffer<int> buffer(storage, 4);
 *
 * buffer.Push(10);
 * buffer.Push(20);
 *
 * int next = 0;
 * if (buffer.Pop(next)) {
 *     // next == 10
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationContainersBitVector BitVector
 *
 * BitVector packs bits eight per byte; bits are read with `Get()` and written
 * with `Set()`, and its capacity counts whole bytes. The bytes live either in
 * an owned `cpstd::vector<uint8_t>` (the default) or in an external buffer:
 * a non-null pointer passed to the constructor or to `Attach()` is always
 * used as storage, and a null pointer selects owned storage.
 *
 * - Owned storage grows on demand. An allocation failure follows
 *   `cpstd::vector`: with CPSTL's own implementation the operation returns
 *   `false` and leaves the bits unchanged; in CPSTL's STL mode it is
 *   `std::vector`'s behavior.
 * - An external buffer is never grown, released or zeroed by the bit vector;
 *   an operation that needs more bytes returns `false` and leaves the bits
 *   unchanged. Copies of a bit vector use owned storage, and moving one takes
 *   its buffer.
 *
 * Operations that change the size may allocate. The library makes no
 * real-time assumptions about the caller: implementers who change the bits
 * in time-critical code reserve the space beforehand with `Reserve()` or use
 * an external buffer.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * uint8_t steps[Foundation::Containers::BitVector::BytesFor(16)] = {};
 * Foundation::Containers::BitVector pattern(steps, sizeof steps);
 * pattern.Resize(16);
 * pattern.Set(0, true);
 * pattern.PushBack(true);   // false: the 16-bit buffer is full
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationContainersOwnership Storage and Lifetime Contract
 *
 * The rules below apply to CircularBuffer and to BitVector while it uses an
 * external buffer.
 *
 * - A container does not own, allocate, resize, or release its backing array.
 * - A null pointer with zero capacity is a valid empty CircularBuffer.
 *   `IsFull()` returns true and insertion fails because no element can fit.
 * - A null pointer with non-zero capacity is an invalid CircularBuffer.
 *   `IsValid()` reports false, `Push()` and `Pop()` return false, and no
 *   pointer is dereferenced.
 * - Non-null storage must remain valid for the complete lifetime of the
 *   container.
 * - The caller must provide at least `capacity` constructed `T` objects because
 *   CircularBuffer insertion uses assignment into existing array elements.
 * - `Push(const T&)` requires copy assignment; `Push(T&&)` and `Pop()` require
 *   move assignment compatible with `cpstd::move`.
 * - `Reset()` clears logical state but does not destroy or overwrite elements.
 * - Operations are not synchronized; external synchronization is required when
 *   one container is shared across execution contexts.
 *
 * @warning The container can validate a null pointer/capacity combination, but
 * it cannot determine the actual length or lifetime of a non-null array. The
 * caller remains responsible for providing at least the declared capacity.
 *
 * @section FoundationContainersExample Runnable Example
 *
 * `Foundation_Containers_DataStructures` demonstrates CircularBuffer
 * wraparound and BitVector packing over an external buffer and over owned
 * storage. See @ref Foundation_Examples "Examples" for build and launch
 * instructions.
 */

/**
 * @namespace Foundation::Containers
 * @ingroup Foundation_Containers
 * @brief Containers the standard library does not provide.
 */
